 "design": "R6 whether the interval is calibrated only at the level we happen to report.\n\nDesign fixed before running (2026-09-16, written 18:05 EDT). Every coverage number in this paper is a 95\npercent number, because that is the level the paper reports. A procedure can be well calibrated at one\nlevel and badly calibrated in the tails, and a reader who wants a 99 percent statement, or who reads the\ninterval as a rough two-sided test at one in ten, gets no guidance from anything we have run. The wild\nbootstrap-t reads percentiles off a simulated distribution, so its tail behaviour is exactly where the\nbootstrap approximation is thinnest.\nThis run scores the same replicates at three nominal levels, one in ten, one in twenty and one in a\nhundred, on the independent population and on the two sharing corners that cost the most coverage, with\n4,000 replicates each. Coverage that tracks the nominal level at all three says the procedure is\ncalibrated rather than tuned. Seed 20260979 keeps the draws distinct.\n",
